   "text": "I read the drawing as a linear peptide: Ac\u2011Ser\u2011Lys\u2011Leu\u2011Arg\u2011(\u03b1\u2011ketobenzothiazole)\u20116\u2011carboxamide\u2011Val\u2011NH2. I wrote it as SMILES in these parts:\n\n- **N\u2011terminus:** an acetyl group on the serine nitrogen, with a CH2OH side chain on serine.\n- **Residues 2 and 3:** lysine with a 4\u2011carbon chain ending in NH2, then leucine with an isobutyl side chain.\n- **Residue 4:** an arginine whose carboxyl is replaced by a ketone. Its side chain is three CH2 groups and a guanidine, and the ketone carbon is bonded to C2 of a benzothiazole.\n- **Benzothiazole:** N3 is attached to the ring-fusion carbon at the top of the drawing. The carboxamide is on C6, para to the ring-fusion carbon next to N3's side\u2026 more precisely, on the benzo carbon reached by going C3a \u2192 C4 \u2192 C5 \u2192 C6. That carboxamide is acylated by the valine nitrogen.\n- **C-terminus:** valine with an isopropyl side chain and a primary amide, C(N)=O.\n\nAll five stereocentres are labelled (S). I assigned each with the usual L\u2011amino\u2011acid pattern, N[C@@H](R)C(=O). For the arginine ketone, the ketone carbon (O,O,C) still outranks the CH2 (C,H,H), so the priorities match an ordinary acid and the same pattern gives S.\n\nSMILES: CC(=O)N[C@@H](CO)C(=O)N[C@@H](CCCCN)C(=O)N[C@@H](CC(C)C)C(=O)N[C@@H](CCCNC(=N)N)C(=O)c1nc2ccc(cc2s1)C(=O)N[C@@H](C(C)C)C(N)=O"
